AMD shared operation packet fixtures
====================================

The reference driver executes AMDComputeQueue/AMDSDMAQueue and amd_build_program
from tinygrad a83c6f8011863c17ef4e9c24c59e521be3c754d3. Only device construction
and submission are bypassed. The OCaml driver executes Encoded_queue.encode;
Queue_fixture reads its actual typed initialization stores. No GPU is opened.
These fixtures establish packet encoding, not hardware execution.

Reproduce from the repository root:
  python3 packages/tolk/test/golden/amdqueue/generate_expected.py \
    --tinygrad _plans/tinygrad-a83c6f801 --output _plans/goldens-a83/amdqueue

The output directory is mandatory; no expected/actual output is used as input.
The reference emits 24 files on gfx1100 and gfx942. Dune checks 23: gfx942 PM4
exec has an explicitly documented scratch-partition divergence (DIVERGENCES.md).
Tolk reserves resident-wave backing per XCC; the target offsets each XCC by
private_segment_size/xccs. The target output remains reproducible, but its words
are not masked into a false parity claim. Runtime tests cover disjoint scratch
slices and the AQL path separately.

The real simple_add_gfx1100.hsaco fixture provides program resource/entry
metadata. Image/scratch/kernargs/signal/write addresses are respectively
0x100000/0x200000/0x300000/0x400000/0x600000. Arguments are
0x900000/0xa00000/0xb00000 and scalar n=32; global/local dimensions are
(4,3,2)/(8,4,1). Device topology is eight CUs and two shader engines per XCC,
64 KiB LDS and four scratch slots per CU. Reference tmpring_size calculation
is invoked directly. The gfx942 configuration has eight XCCs and uses the
plain PM4 encoder for these tests; it does not imply that this gfx1100 binary
can execute on gfx942.

Migration attribution
---------------------
Raven 60067122d introduced the old HCQBuffer/ArgsState corpus at baa6148066.
Shared HCQ2 signal/timestamp packets no longer use a separate owner-specific
queue-event mailbox path. The two signal/timestamp pairs and two SDMA signals
therefore change. SDMA gfx942 large-copy chunks use the 1 GiB limit from
upstream 525b490c1 (the revision predicate also excludes SDMA 5.0/5.1).
The exec pair also changes because metadata now comes from a
real ELF and the target scratch calculation, rather than manually copied fields.

Retired compute write32/write64/poll_bit cases (six files), owner-specific
signal_timeline/sdma_signal_timeline cases (four files), and synthetic
exec_scratch_gfx1100 are removed: the target no longer exposes those methods.
The shared SDMA write instruction remains supported, including mixed 32/64-bit
patches and replay. Its payloads are generated with the target AMDSDMAQueue.write,
not the retired direct Copy_queue.write helper. Existing AQL/dispatch-pointer and
scratch runtime tests cover protocol behavior outside this dword corpus.

Format: one lowercase eight-digit dword per line, trailing newline.
